That's not really necessary. I've got at least all
the types of (save compatible appropriate) bones
file types that Alexis/Hearse has, and almost
certainly more, since ones of types of which I've
kept copies may no longer be available on Hearse due
to the Hearse bones file expiry mechanism.

But just by usual statistical distribution, plus
perhaps an aversion by a majority of players to some
PC types, there just aren't any current bones files
for the types I'm missing compatible with my WinXP
vanilla release playing platform. As of the last
time I tried, for example, there was only one of the
four Rogue quest potential bones files, two of the
Knight quest ones, three of a few other quests, and
two Gehennom ordinary levels missing (maybe, I'm not
sure the consecutive list can all really drop
bones files).

That's also not really in concert with my plan,
which is to come by the files "honestly", in
exchange for bones files I contribute to Hearse.

Believe me, I drop enough bones files easily to
download a full kit of bones files in exchange
through normal operations. Not counting the 680 or
so PC deaths before I started doing strictly random
selections and naming them sequentially, I've now
murdered off PCs Rnd00001 - Rnd00167 as well.

[Notice the cheerful optimism in which I allowed
for five digits to count the slaughter before I
give up on randomly chosen PCs.]

Of course "honestly" is stretched rather broadly by
my using Hearse in "keep" mode so that I don't
delete the bones file copy I contribute, and by my
retaining a huge "backbones" directory from which I
can replace bones files that get consumed but not
while dropping more bones for that level.

The end result is that I'm not playing the bones
file deprived game others are playing, I'm playing a
game either much richer or much harder, depending on
your attitude toward a bones file with some
sufficiently nasty killer but also some sufficiently
nice treasure might be.

I'm playing the game for my own pleasure, not to
compete with anyone but myself

         [which is why I usually set aside games that
         look ascendible; my save directory just now
         contains 48 such games being ignored, the
         oldest from 10/2004; I'm _good_ at ignoring
         stuff I don't want to confront, that's part
         of what "uncured acute monopolar depression"
         means]

and I like having to "solve" each bones file; I've
got an especially nice toplevel mines bones file
right now in which a rabid rat will be with me in
five moves, so I can't enter mines (and stay for
more than four moves) without some solution for the
rabid rat problem. I've pretty much solved that one,
it isn't a very hard problem, but the bones files at
mu "current use" level are all good practice for me
in that I can keep confronting them in game after
game until I get them right (or drop a newer bones
file to bury them, my backbones directory contains
pushdown stacks of bones file types).

xanthian, obsessive person.


